# Cursor Rules — diegops

## Project

`diegops` is a cross-platform personal productivity CLI written in Rust.

### Target platforms
- `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` — Windows (WSL and native, GNU toolchain)
- `x86_64-apple-darwin` — macOS Intel
- `aarch64-apple-darwin` — macOS Apple Silicon
- `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` — Ubuntu / Debian
- `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl` — Alpine (static binary)
- `aarch64-unknown-linux-musl` — ARM64 Alpine (static binary)

### Stack
- Rust stable, clap v4 (derive), Cargo
- GitHub Actions: CI on every push, releases on `vx.y.z` tags
- All `runs-on` must be `[cadi-hq-runner-dind-set-v2, cadi-hq-runner-dind-set]` (self-hosted Linux dind) — never GitHub-hosted runners
- Build tools: native cargo (linux-gnu), cargo-zigbuild/Zig (all other targets — no Docker/cross needed)
- Windows target: `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` — never `msvc` (impossible to cross-compile from Linux)
- All cross-compilation via Zig as linker; Zig ships musl libc and MinGW

---

## Mandatory rules

### 1. Cross-platform paths
Use `std::path::PathBuf` — never string concatenation for paths.

### 2. No `.unwrap()` in production code
Use `?` operator. Only `.unwrap()` in tests and with a `// SAFETY:` comment if truly infallible.

### 3. OS guards for platform-specific code
```rust
#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]
fn windows_thing() { ... }
#[cfg(not(target_os = "windows"))]
fn unix_thing() { ... }
```

### 4. No shell invocation without OS detection
Do not assume `sh`, `bash`, `cmd.exe`, or `powershell` exists.

### 5. Idempotency
Every command is safe to run multiple times. No errors on already-existing state. "Already done" exits 0.

```rust
// Wrong: fails on second run
fs::create_dir(&path)?;
// Right
fs::create_dir_all(&path)?;
```

### 6. Exit codes
- `0` = success (including idempotent no-ops)
- `1` = user/input error
- `2` = internal/unexpected error
`main()` returns `Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>` and uses `?`. No `std::process::exit()` outside `main`.

### 7. stdout vs stderr
- `println!` → data / machine-readable output (stdout)
- `eprintln!` → errors, warnings, diagnostics (stderr)
Never mix. This makes `diegops cmd | jq` and CI log capture work correctly.

### 8. TTY / container awareness
The binary must work without a TTY (Docker, CI pipelines):
- No interactive prompts — use flags/args.
- Custom ANSI color: check `std::io::IsTerminal` before emitting escape codes.
- `clap` auto-respects `NO_COLOR`.
- Do not assume stdin is a terminal.

### 9. Code quality
Code must pass:
- `cargo fmt --check`
- `cargo clippy -- -D warnings`
- `cargo test`

### 10. Imports
Remove all unused imports. Keep `std` as the first choice over external crates.

### 11. Error handling
`main()` returns `Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>`. Command handlers return `Result<T, E>`. Propagate with `?`.

### 12. Testing
- Unit tests inline with `#[cfg(test)]`
- Integration tests in `tests/` using `env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_diegops")`
- No network calls in tests
- Tests must work on all target platforms
- Verify exit codes: check `output.status.success()` or `output.status.code()`

### 13. Binary size discipline
The release profile uses `opt-level = "z"`, `lto = true`, `strip = true`. Don't add heavy dependencies for simple tasks.

---

## Commands

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `diegops version` | Print version string |
| `diegops update` | Self-update from latest GitHub release |
| `diegops repo init` | Create sample config at `~/.diegops/repos.yaml` |
| `diegops repo apply [--path PREFIX] [--config FILE]` | Clone all missing repos (idempotent) |
| `diegops repo list [--config FILE]` | Show repos cloned locally |
| `diegops repo list-diff [--config FILE]` | Show repos in config but not cloned |
| `diegops help` | Show full help |

`diegops update`: target triple via compile-time `#[cfg]` constants; HTTP via `ureq` (rustls, sync); archive via `flate2`+`tar` (Unix) / `zip` (Windows); binary replacement via atomic rename (Unix) or rename-to-`.old` (Windows). Network-dependent — only test `--help` offline.

## Structure conventions

```
src/main.rs              # Thin CLI dispatcher — parse args, call commands
src/commands/mod.rs      # Module declarations
src/commands/<name>.rs   # Non-trivial command logic (extract when >20 lines)
tests/integration_test.rs
Cargo.toml
Cargo.lock               # Always committed
```

## Do not
- Use nightly Rust features
- Hardcode home directory paths
- Use `std::process::exit()` except in `main` as a last resort
- Commit the `target/` directory
- Add async unless the command genuinely needs concurrent I/O
- Use `native-tls` / OpenSSL — use `ureq` with `default-features = false, features = ["tls", "json"]` (rustls) for musl/static compatibility
